											This linked to Account/Logout, which signs out the INTERNAL employee identity and
											leaves the portal cookie standing. On a shared machine a customer signing out of
											the portal would have signed a colleague out of the ERP instead, and stayed
											signed in themselves. PortalAccount/Logout names the CrossBuy.Portal scheme and
											removes only that cookie.

											A FORM RATHER THAN A LINK because the action is [HttpPost] and anti-forgery
											protected, which it must be: signing somebody out is a mutation, and a GET that
											performs one can be fired by any image tag on any site.
										*@
										<div class="menu-item px-5">
											<form method="post" action="@Url.Action("Logout", "PortalAccount")" class="m-0">
												@Html.AntiForgeryToken()
												<button type="submit" class="menu-link px-5 btn btn-link text-start w-100 p-0 border-0 bg-transparent">@(isAr ? "تسجيل الخروج" : "Sign Out")</button>
											</form>
										</div>
